{"authored" [{"question" "q-114015" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,$ calculate\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/state.json" "file_sha256" "e08a60b4d97f3d47007b4efdb7f7fd606625bb3eb2098cf82658b868fe3b7300" "basis" 1106 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114015" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can split up the given integral using the sum rule and factor out the constants. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(f(x)-5)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x-5{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot x\\,{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =1-5(2-0) \\\\  & =1-5\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/state.json" "file_sha256" "e08a60b4d97f3d47007b4efdb7f7fd606625bb3eb2098cf82658b868fe3b7300" "basis" 1106 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114015" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e0366f23d3812e03904accf1acab42f1872d82ef69e7c77814658267bfe22812" "basis" 1106 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004887/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-114015" "field" "selection" "att